window.__labLoaded({"id":"1.3","title":"Variance and Standard Deviation","goal":"Compute variance and standard deviation for the lesson's five quiz scores by hand and with NumPy, and see the population versus sample difference.","question":"A teacher has five quiz scores: 72, 85, 90, 68 and 95. The average is 82, but she wants one number for how spread out the scores are. She also wonders why her software offers two different standard deviations, and which one to trust.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"By hand: the six steps","text":"Follow the lesson: find the mean, the deviations, square them, add them up (the sum of squares), divide by n - 1 and take the square root. The raw deviations always add to zero.","code":"import numpy as np\n\nscore = np.array([72, 85, 90, 68, 95])\n\nmean = score.sum() / len(score)\ndev = score - mean\nss = (dev ** 2).sum()\nvar = ss / (len(score) - 1)\n\nprint('mean =', mean, ' deviations =', dev, ' sum =', dev.sum())\nprint('sum of squares =', ss)\nprint(f'sample variance = {var:.1f}, sample sd = {np.sqrt(var):.2f}')","explain":["Load NumPy, the library for lists of numbers, as np.","Store the five quiz scores in an array so we can do arithmetic on all of them at once.","Step 1, the mean: total divided by the count. This is the centre the spread is measured from.","Step 2, the deviations: how far each score sits from the mean. NumPy subtracts the mean from all five at once.","Steps 3 and 4: square each deviation (this removes minus signs) and add them up. This is the sum of squares.","Step 5, the variance: divide by n - 1, not n, because a sample sits closer to its own mean than to the true mean, so dividing by n would under-estimate the spread.","Print the mean, the deviations and their sum. The sum is zero, which is why we cannot average raw deviations.","Print the sum of squares.","Step 6: the square root brings the variance back to score units. That gives the standard deviation."],"meaning":"The mean is 82. The deviations -10, 3, 8, -14 and 13 add to 0, so they must be squared. The sum of squares is 538, the sample variance is 538 / 4 = 134.5, and the sample standard deviation is 11.60 points: a typical score lies about 11.6 points from 82.","out":"mean = 82.0  deviations = [-10.   3.   8. -14.  13.]  sum = 0.0\nsum of squares = 538.0\nsample variance = 134.5, sample sd = 11.60","figs":[]},{"title":"The same with NumPy: ddof 0 or 1","text":"NumPy lets you choose the divisor with ddof (delta degrees of freedom). ddof=1 divides by n - 1 (a sample), ddof=0 divides by n (a whole population).","code":"print(f'sample     variance = {score.var(ddof=1):.1f}, sd = {score.std(ddof=1):.2f}')\nprint(f'population variance = {score.var(ddof=0):.1f}, sd = {score.std(ddof=0):.2f}')","explain":["ddof=1 divides by n - 1. Use it when the scores are a sample from a bigger group.","ddof=0 divides by n. Use it only when you hold every member of the group."],"meaning":"The sample version gives variance 134.5 and SD 11.60, matching our hand work. The population version gives variance 107.6 and SD 10.37. The sample version is bigger on purpose, to correct for samples looking less spread out than the whole group. With five scores the difference is large, and with hundreds it almost vanishes.","out":"sample     variance = 134.5, sd = 11.60\npopulation variance = 107.6, sd = 10.37","figs":[]},{"title":"A trap: NumPy and pandas differ","text":"np.std defaults to ddof=0 but pandas .std() defaults to ddof=1. Always say which one you mean. R, SPSS and Stata all use the sample version (n - 1) by default.","code":"import pandas as pd\n\ns = pd.Series(score)\nprint('np.std(score)     =', round(np.std(score), 2), '(population)')\nprint('pd.Series.std()   =', round(s.std(), 2), '(sample)')","explain":["Load pandas, Python's tool for tables and columns of data, as pd.","Wrap the scores in a pandas Series (a column of data) to see what pandas does by default.","np.std defaults to ddof=0, the population version.","pandas .std() defaults to ddof=1, the sample version."],"meaning":"For the same five numbers np.std gives 10.37 and pandas gives 11.6, because their defaults differ. Neither is wrong, but you must know which you used and say so. R, SPSS and Stata all use the sample version (n - 1) by default.","out":"np.std(score)     = 10.37 (population)\npd.Series.std()   = 11.6 (sample)","figs":[]},{"title":"Same mean, different spread","text":"Dataset A is perfectly steady and B swings widely, yet both have mean 50. The standard deviation tells them apart. Here B is treated as a whole population (divide by N).","code":"A = np.array([50, 50, 50, 50])\nB = np.array([10, 30, 70, 90])\n\nprint('means:', A.mean(), B.mean())\nprint('sd of A:', A.std(ddof=0))\nprint(f'sd of B: {B.std(ddof=0):.2f}  (variance {B.var(ddof=0):.0f})')","explain":["Dataset A: four identical values. Nothing varies.","Dataset B: four values far from 50 on both sides, but with the same mean as A.","Print both means to show they are equal.","The SD of A, treating it as a complete group (ddof=0).","The SD and variance of B. :.0f shows the variance as a whole number."],"meaning":"Both datasets have a mean of 50, but A's SD is 0.0 and B's is 31.62 (variance 1000). The mean cannot tell them apart and the SD can. An SD of exactly 0 happens only when every value is identical.","out":"means: 50.0 50.0\nsd of A: 0.0\nsd of B: 31.62  (variance 1000)","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"score <- c(72, 85, 90, 68, 95)\nmean(score)\nvar(score)\nsd(score)\n# population version: divide by n instead of n - 1\nvar(score) * (length(score) - 1) / length(score)\nsqrt(var(score) * (length(score) - 1) / length(score))","explain":["Store the five quiz scores in a vector.","mean() gives the centre of the scores.","var() gives the sample variance, using the divisor n - 1.","sd() gives the sample standard deviation, the square root of that variance.","A comment: R ignores everything after #. We use it to note that the next lines change the divisor.","R has no built-in population variance, so we convert: undo the n - 1 divisor and divide by n instead.","The square root of that population variance is the population standard deviation."],"expect":"mean 82; var 134.5; sd 11.59741 (11.6); population variance 107.6, population sd 10.37","read":"R prints 82 (mean), 134.5 (variance) and 11.59741 (SD). These are the sample versions. The last two lines print the population variance 107.6 and the population SD 10.37.","note":"var() and sd() in R are the sample versions (divide by n - 1), the same as ddof=1. R has no built-in population version, so we rescale."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Descriptives > Options","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / score.\nBEGIN DATA\n72  85  90  68  95\nEND DATA.\nDESCRIPTIVES VARIABLES=score\n  /STATISTICS=MEAN STDDEV VARIANCE.","explain":["Tell SPSS we type the data in the syntax, in free format, into one variable called score.","BEGIN DATA starts the data lines.","The five quiz scores, separated by spaces.","END DATA ends the data lines.","Descriptives is the command for mean, standard deviation and variance of a numerical variable.","Ask for exactly those three statistics, and finish the command with a full stop."],"read":"In the Descriptive Statistics table read Mean 82.00, Std. Deviation 11.597 and Variance 134.500. These are the sample (n - 1) versions. SPSS has no option for the population version.","note":"SPSS always reports the sample standard deviation and variance (n - 1): 11.597 and 134.500. It has no option for the population version."},"stata":{"code":"clear\ninput score\n72\n85\n90\n68\n95\nend\nsummarize score, detail\ndisplay r(Var) * (r(N) - 1) / r(N)","explain":["Clear any old data from memory.","Start typing the data by hand into a variable called score.","First score, one value per row: 72.","Second score: 85.","Third score: 90.","Fourth score: 68.","Fifth score: 95.","end tells Stata that the typed data are finished.","summarize with detail prints the mean, variance and standard deviation, all the sample versions.","r(Var) and r(N) are results stored by summarize. This line rescales the sample variance to the population one."],"read":"summarize prints Mean 82, Std. Dev. 11.59741 and Variance 134.5, all with the n - 1 divisor. The display line prints 107.6, the population variance.","note":"summarize reports the sample variance 134.5 and Std. Dev. 11.597 (n - 1). The display line rescales the variance to the population version, 107.6."},"conclusion":"The five scores average 82 and their sample standard deviation is 11.6, so a typical score is about 11.6 points from the mean (variance 134.5, in squared points). Use n - 1 for a sample, and n only when you hold the entire group. Caution: with five numbers the SD is a rough estimate, and one extreme score would change it a lot.","practice":{"scenario":"A factory has two machines that fill 500 ml bottles. The quality manager measures six bottles from each machine. Both machines average 500 ml, but she suspects one is much less steady than the other.","data":"machine_a = [499, 501, 500, 502, 498, 500]; machine_b = [490, 510, 495, 505, 500, 500]","tasks":["Find the mean of each machine.","Find the sample standard deviation of each machine.","Say which machine is steadier, and by how many times.","Compute the population SDs too, and say which version suits this problem."],"hint":"Use np.mean and np.std with ddof=1. A sample of six bottles is not every bottle the machine will ever fill.","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\n\na = np.array([499, 501, 500, 502, 498, 500])\nb = np.array([490, 510, 495, 505, 500, 500])\nfor name, x in [('A', a), ('B', b)]:\n    print(f'machine {name}: mean = {x.mean():.1f}, sample sd = {x.std(ddof=1):.2f}, '\n          f'population sd = {x.std(ddof=0):.2f}')\nprint(f'variance of B (sample) = {b.var(ddof=1):.1f}')","answer":"Both machines average 500.0 ml. Machine A has a sample SD of 1.41 ml and machine B 7.07 ml, five times larger, so B is far less steady even though its mean is perfect. The population SDs (1.29 and 6.45) are smaller. Use the sample SD here, because six bottles are only a sample of what each machine fills.","out":"machine A: mean = 500.0, sample sd = 1.41, population sd = 1.29\nmachine B: mean = 500.0, sample sd = 7.07, population sd = 6.45\nvariance of B (sample) = 50.0","figs":[]}}});
